 "endReason": "concluded",
 "customerTurns": 3,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I think my washer dryer has a fault - I put it on just a drying programme with dry-ish clothes and I can hear water running into it and draining the whole time. Surely it shouldn't use water to dry? It's a Beko."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is the cold water supply the condenser needs. Most washer-dryers use a trickle of cold water to condense the moisture while drying, so the cold tap must stay on and the hose mustn't be kinked. Was the tap off or the hose kinked (and is it sorted), or is the water supply on and fine?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "The tap is on and the hose isn't kinked as far as I can tell, and there's no leak anywhere. The clothes come out dry and there are no error codes. I just didn't expect a dryer to use water at all - my old tumble dryer was a vented one."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Does it get hot during drying but leave the clothes damp, or is there no heat at all - and were the clothes properly spun before the drying stage?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "The clothes come out dry, so nothing is actually failing - it dries well and the time counts down normally. I only wanted to know whether it's normal for it to use water at all. I did look in the manual and it says water is used during drying for condensation."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, it is normal for Beko tumble dryers with a heat pump or specific condenser systems to use water during the drying cycle. The manual confirms that water is used to manage condensation and humidity within the machine. From what you have described, the most likely cause is confirm the complaint is genuine failure, not normal heat-pump behaviour. Is it genuinely cold and never drying, or does it dry but just feels cooler / slower than your old dryer?"
  }
 ],
